<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-16">16 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Soyuz 4 and Soyuz 5 make the first crewed docking</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Two Soviet spacecraft docked in orbit and two cosmonauts spacewalked from Soyuz 5 to Soyuz 4. It was the first exchange of crew between spacecraft, a rehearsal for building space stations.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Concorde makes its first flight</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Test pilot André Turcat flew the Anglo-French supersonic airliner prototype from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. Concorde entered passenger service in 1976 and flew until 2003.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-10">10 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">James Earl Ray pleads guilty to killing Martin Luther King Jr.</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Ray was sentenced to 99 years in prison. Days later he recanted his plea and sought a trial for the rest of his life, fuelling persistent claims of a conspiracy.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Charles de Gaulle resigns as President of France</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After voters rejected his referendum on regional and Senate reform, de Gaulle stepped down at noon. He had led the Fifth Republic since its foundation in 1958.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-26">26 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Apollo 10 splashes down after the Moon landing rehearsal</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> 10 returned safely after its lunar module descended to about 15 km above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a>. The mission tested every step of a landing except the touchdown itself.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-16">16 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Apollo 11 launches for the Moon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A Saturn V rocket lifted Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-collins">Michael Collins</a> from Kennedy Space Center. Four days later Armstrong and Aldrin became the first people to land on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Apollo 11 lands on the Moon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed the lunar module Eagle in the Sea of Tranquillity. Hours later, at 10.56 pm US Eastern time, Armstrong stepped onto the surface, watched by some 600 million people.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-21">21 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Armstrong and Aldrin lift off from the Moon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After about 21 hours on the lunar surface, Armstrong and Aldrin fired the ascent stage of Eagle to rejoin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-collins">Michael Collins</a> in lunar orbit. They left a plaque reading 'We came in peace for all mankind'.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-9">9 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Manson Family members murder Sharon Tate and four others</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Followers of Charles Manson killed actress Sharon Tate, who was heavily pregnant, and four others at her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> home. The murders horrified America and Manson became a byword for evil.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-1">1 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Muammar Gaddafi seizes power in Libya</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Junior army officers led by 27-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a> overthrew King Idris in a bloodless coup. Gaddafi ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> for 42 years until his overthrow and death in 2011.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Apollo 12 launches for the Moon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> 12 survived two lightning strikes during launch from Cape Kennedy. Five days later Pete Conrad and Alan Bean landed precisely beside the Surveyor 3 probe in the Ocean of Storms.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/football-war">Football War</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Football War was a brief war between El Salvador and Honduras in July 1969, also called the Hundred Hours' War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/corrective-move">Corrective Move</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Corrective Move was the seizure of power on 22 June 1969 by the left wing of South Yemen's ruling National Liberation Front, which removed President Qahtan al-Shaabi.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/act-of-free-choice">Act of Free Choice</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Act of Free Choice was the process in July and August 1969 by which Indonesia sought to confirm its sovereignty over West New Guinea under the New York Agreement of 1962.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/operation-sheepskin">Operation Sheepskin</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Operation Sheepskin was the British landing on Anguilla on 19 March 1969 to end the island's self-declared break from the associated state of Saint Kitts-Nevis-Anguilla.</p></div></li></ol></section>
